## Infrastructure Specifics

- Firewalls: Block unauthorized traffic and protect against attacks.
- SSL Certificate: Enables encrypted communications between the user and the server, protecting sensitive data.
- Monitoring Clients: Collect metrics on resource usage, response times, etc., to detect issues and optimize performance.
- Data Collection: Monitoring clients use agents installed on servers to collect data, which is then sent to the centralized monitoring service.
- QPS Monitoring: To monitor the QPS (Requests Per Second) of the web server, the monitoring client will collect data on the number of requests processed by the web server per unit of time.

## Persistent Issues
- SSL Termination at the Load Balancer: This exposes decrypted traffic between the load balancer and the web servers, which can be a security risk if the internal network is not secured.
- Single MySQL master node: If the master node fails, data writes are not possible, which can lead to service interruption.
- Homogeneous servers: If all servers have the same components, a vulnerability in one can affect all the others, increasing the risk of a widespread outage.